# UST.G3FJ - H02 Component-20 K3 Normal Matrix-Factorization Theorem

## Result

For the frozen q79 degree-two K3 and the selected root graph normals

```text
q    = xz-y^2,
r    = w+G3,
rbar = w-G3,
```

there is an explicit integral quartic `H4` such that

```text
K3 = r*rbar + q*H4.
```

The quartic has 13 nonzero monomial coefficients and is coprime to `q`.

## Matrix factorization

Define

```text
M = [[r,   q],
     [-H4, rbar]],

N = [[rbar, -q],
     [H4,    r]].
```

Direct polynomial multiplication proves

```text
M*N = K3*I2,
N*M = K3*I2.
```

On the K3 hypersurface, the columns of `N` therefore provide two exact,
denominator-free normal syzygies:

```text
(rbar,H4)  and  (-q,r).
```

## Binding to the selected source

UST.G3FI constructed the selected normal pair `(B6,D7)`. G3FJ verifies in
all six coordinates of `Q(gamma)` that

```text
A0_selected-A1 = [r q] * (B6,D7)^T.
```

The component-20 source is therefore a section of the exact K3 normal
matrix-factorization module. This is stronger than merely knowing that it
vanishes after restriction to the root graph.

## Global boundary

On the selected branch, `rbar=-2*G3`. It is not a global unit on the
projective conic. Consequently one may not construct the homotopy by dividing
by `w-G3`. The matrix factorization is the correct global, denominator-free
replacement.

## What remains

The matrix factorization supplies the normal-transfer mechanism but does not
yet select the 100-dimensional lower-normal plane. The next proof must lift
the two-periodic contraction into the frozen H02 Cox degree, truncate it
through the four conic layers, and replay all 100 resulting relations against
the exact G3EX presentation. A separate nonzero 36 by 36 residual minor is
still required for rank equality.

